_PSB-781_2.raw\nE33218_1p_uPAC22_PM4_PSB-781_3_20230922172934.raw\nE33220_1p_uPAC22_PM4_PSB-781_4.raw\nE33222_1p_uPAC22_PM4_PSB-781_5.raw\nE33224_1p_uPAC22_PM4_PSB-781_6.raw\nE33541_2p_uPAC21_PM6_PSB-817_4.raw\nE33543_2p_uPAC21_PM6_PSB-817_5.raw\nE33545_2p_uPAC21_PM6_PSB-817_6.raw\nE33547_2p_uPAC21_PM6_PSB-817_7.raw\nE33549_2p_uPAC21_PM6_PSB-817_8.raw\nE33551_2p_uPAC21_PM6_PSB-817_9.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nA Nitrogen-specific Interactome Analysis Sheds Light on the Role of the SnRK1 and TOR Kinases in\nPlant Nitrogen Signaling part1 AP-MS and TurboID\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nNitrogen (N) is of utmost importance for plant growth and development. Multiple studies have shown that N signaling is tightly coupled with carbon (C) levels, but the interplay between C/N metabolism and growth remains largely an enigma. Nonetheless, the protein kinases sucrose non-fermenting 1 (SNF1)-related kinase 1 (SnRK1) and target of rapamycin (TOR), two ancient central metabolic regulators, are emerging as key integrators that link C/N status with growth. Despite their pivotal importance, the exact mechanisms behind the sensing of N status and its integration with C availability to drive metabolic decisions are largely unknown. Especially for SnRK1 it is not clear how this kinase responds to altered N levels. Therefore, we first monitored N-dependent SnRK1 kinase activity at high resolution with our in vivo separation of phase-based activity reporter of kinase (SPARK), revealing a contrasting N-dependency in shoot and root tissues. Next, using affinity purification (AP) and proximity labeling (PL) coupled to mass spectrometry (MS) experiments, we constructed an N-specific SnRK1 and TOR interactome in Arabidopsis thaliana cell cultures during N-starved and\xa0 repleted growth conditions. To broaden our understanding of the N-dependency of the TOR/SnRK1 signaling pathway, the resulting network was compared to corresponding C-related networks, identifying a large number of novel, N-specific interactors. Moreover, through integration of N dependent transcriptome and phosphoproteome data, we were able to pinpoint additional N dependent network components, revealing SnRK1 regulatory proteins that might function at the crosstalk of C/N signaling. Finally, confirmation of known and identification of novel interactors, such as IRE1A and RAB18, indicate that the endoplasmic reticulum functions as a key regulatory hub for TOR and SnRK1 via integration of C/N signaling.\n\n[METHODS]\nArabidopsis Cell Culture Transformation and Biomass Generation for Interactomics All GSrhino and linkerTurboID-3xHA fusion constructs were transferred to A. tumefaciens C58C1 RifR (pMP90), transformed into the dark-growing Arabidopsis PSB-D cell culture and upscaled in standard nutrient-rich conditions. Three days after subculturing, cells were washed and resuspended in N-free medium containing 0.5 % (w/v) sucrose. After 48 h of N starvation, cultures were split into two shake flasks, and 1 mM KNO3 was added to one while the other served as N-starvation control. For the TurboID cultures, 50 uM biotin was added 48 h after N starvation, and cells were grown further for 15 min or 1\xa0h with or without KNO3. Cells were harvested through vacuum infiltration, sampling 7,5 gram of PSB-D cells for triplicate AP-MS experiments, or 9 grams of PSB-D cells for triplicate PL-MS experiments, and harvested cell material was frozen in N2 and stored at -70°C. Isolation of Protein Complexes by GSrhino-Based Affinity Purification GSrhino-based affinity purification (AP) experiments were performed in triplicate starting from 2.5 g of frozen cell material per replicate (Van Leene et al., 2019). Briefly, protein complexes were trapped through the Protein G part of the GSrhino tag by incubating 25 mg total protein extract per replicate for 45 min with 50 uL in-house prepared magnetic immunoglobulin G (IgG) bead suspension. Beads were washed three times with 500 uL extraction buffer, once with 500 uL extraction buffer without detergent, and once with 800 uL 50 mM NH4HCO3 (pH 8.0). The wash buffer was removed and beads were incubated in 50 uL 50 mM NH4HCO3 supplemented with 1 g Trypsin/Lys-C (Promega) for 4 h at 37°C. Next, the digest was separated from the beads and further incubated overnight with 0.5 g Trypsin/Lys-C at 37°C. Finally, the digest was centrifuged at 20,800 rcf for 5 min, and supernatant was dried in a SpeedVac and stored at 20°C until MS analysis.  TurboID-Based Proximity Labeling (PL) Proximity labeling experiments were performed from 3g harvested cell material per replicate, as described in detail for Arabidopsis PSB-D cells (Arora et al., 2020), using denaturing extraction buffer (100 mM Tris-HCl (pH 7.5), 2% (w/v) SDS, 8 M urea) and with the additional acid elution step following the Trypsin/Lys-C digest, as described in (Van Leene et al., 2022).  LC-MS/MS Analysis On-bead digested samples were analyzed on a Q Exactive (ThermoFisher Scientific) using standard procedures. Briefly, peptides were redissolved in loading solvent A (0.1% TFA in water/ACN (98:2, v/v)) and injected for LC-MS/MS analysis on an Ultimate 3000 RSLC nano LC (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Bremen, Germany) in-line connected to a Q Exactive mass spectrometer. The peptides were first loaded on a trapping column (made in-house, 100\x1eum internal diameter (I.D.) × 20 mm, 5\x1eum beads C18 Reprosil-HD, Dr. Maisch, Ammerbuch-Entringen, Germany), and after flushing from the trapping column, the peptides were separated on a 50\x1ecm µPAC™ column with C18-endcapped functionality (Pharmafluidics, Belgium) kept at a constant temperature of 50°C. Peptides were eluted by a nonlinear gradient from 99% solvent A\' (0.1% formic acid in water) ) to 10% solvent B′ (0.1% formic acid in water/ACN, 20/80 (v/v)) in 9 min, 30% solvent B’ in 29 min, 50% solvent B’ in 34 min at a flow rate of 300 nL/min, followed by a 5 min wash reaching 95% solvent B’. The mass spectrometer was operated in data-dependent, positive ionization mode, automatically switching between MS and MS/MS acquisition for the five most abundant peaks in a given MS spectrum.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nArabidopsis thaliana (mouse-ear cress)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
